# DIAGNOSTICS

fs prints why it stopped, `no partition PATH` or `no volume on PATH`, and exits 1.
A call the service doesn't do yet fails with `unsupported`.

# BUGS

There are no permissions, owners or symbolic links over Node.
Files open when fs restarts stay closed; `/data` itself reconnects to the new fs (launcher(8)).
Free space is a bitmap held in memory six times over (in use, and blocks held, deferred, retired, allocated and logged in the current groups): about 180 MB on a 1 TB partition.
A commit's work on the bitmap grows with what changed since the last, except the first after the volume is mounted, which hashes the whole bitmap (about 40 ms a terabyte on the dev machine); requests wait while a commit runs.
Queries on `size` and `mtime` see a write behind only once it is written back, within a second.
A write that fails when it is written back (a disk error, or no space after all) stays in memory and is tried again at the next commit; only the sync that met it says so.
`--check` takes about a millisecond a node on the Orange Pi 6 Plus (11 s for 11,253), and the volume's clients wait for it at every boot.
A snapshot is served by Node alone: it has no fids, rings, attributes through the fs protocol, queries or mapped files, and every read goes to the disk, past the files' VMOs.
Nothing deletes snapshots on their own: with them, a volume can fill, and its writes fail with `no space` until one is deleted.
Two snapshots' files can share a qid path, when their txgs are 32768 apart.
